WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
Nitrogen gas makes up most of the air around every plant.

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.500
So why can a pea use it, while an oat beside it starves?

3
00:00:12.483 --> 00:00:17.443
The first film showed that how far a nutrient moves shapes how a plant reaches it.

4
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:19.803
But form matters too.

5
00:00:21.243 --> 00:00:24.943
Nitrogen gas is everywhere, yet no plant enzyme can use it.

6
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:30.877
At an experiment station in Bernburg, Hermann Hellriegel and Hermann Wilfarth tested

7
00:00:30.877 --> 00:00:36.037
this in pots. They grew peas and oats in washed, sterilised sand.

8
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.080
Every pot got a base solution with the other mineral nutrients, but no nitrogen.

9
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.380
So any extra nitrogen had to come from what they added, or from the air.

10
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.363
Now think about what that means.

11
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:56.783
If every plant relied on soil nitrogen alone, none could end up with more nitrogen

12
00:00:56.783 --> 00:00:57.783
than its seed held.

13
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:01.177
Some oat pots got no nitrogen at all.

14
00:01:02.617 --> 00:01:04.317
Others got measured doses of nitrate.

15
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:08.617
The oats grew only as far as the nitrate they were given allowed.

16
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.000
Peas with nothing added did the same, and stayed small.

17
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.600
But some peas got a little extract shaken from field soil.

18
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:19.900
Those grew well.

19
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:23.233
Their roots formed swellings called nodules.

20
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:28.353
And the plants ended up with far more nitrogen than their seeds had held.

21
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:31.593
That extra nitrogen came from the air.

22
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:36.677
One more set of pots got soil extract that had been heated first.

23
00:01:37.937 --> 00:01:40.497
Will those peas form nodules, and gain nitrogen?

24
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.400
No. No nodules, and no nitrogen gained.

25
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.980
A living agent in the soil, tied to the nodules, was needed.

26
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.370
That agent is a group of bacteria called rhizobia.

27
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:02.730
They enter the root.

28
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.090
Inside nodule cells, they change into bacteroids.

29
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.050
Bacteroids hold an enzyme called nitrogenase.

30
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.110
It turns nitrogen gas into ammonia, a form the plant can use.

31
00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.390
The pea itself makes no nitrogenase.

32
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:21.883
The pea pays for this.

33
00:02:23.223 --> 00:02:25.983
Sugar made in its leaves travels down to the nodule.

34
00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:30.483
There it is turned into malate, which fuels the bacteroids.

35
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:33.513
So the trade is carbon for nitrogen.

36
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:36.113
Oats have no nodules.

37
00:02:37.353 --> 00:02:41.013
They depend mainly on nitrate and ammonium already in the soil.

38
00:02:42.117 --> 00:02:46.497
Nitrogen gas is all around the pea, but in a form it cannot use.

39
00:02:47.697 --> 00:02:50.117
So it pays carbon to a partner that can use it.
